\pard\sb200\sa80\b\fs22 18. Data Lifecycle and Compliance Tools\b0\fs20\par
\pard\sb60\sa60 The Software includes advanced features for data management and access control. \b These features are provided strictly as compliance, privacy, and safety tools.\b0\par
\pard\sb120\sa60\b 18.1 Local Endpoint Sanitization (Secure Erase)\b0\par
\pard\fi-240\li480\sb40 - \b Function:\b0  secure-erase features execute a cryptographically-random single-pass overwrite on designated local storage, consistent with NIST SP 800-88 guidance for magnetic (HDD) media (pass count is configurable up to 7 for legacy compliance policies, though additional passes add no measurable benefit on modern drives and are automatically skipped on SSD/NVMe/flash media, where cryptographic erasure of the encryption key is the effective sanitization method), and privacy-cleanup features remove residual local artifacts and traces.\par
- \b Intended Use:\b0  to protect data confidentiality on the physical endpoint in the event of \b theft, loss, coercion, or other unauthorized physical access\b0 , in conjunction with an offsite backup strategy to preserve data availability. It is \b not\b0  intended to, and must not be used to, destroy evidence subject to legal hold or to obstruct lawful process.\par
- \b Cleanup Disclaimer:\b0  ServaLabs makes no guarantee regarding absolute cleanup irrecoverability, particularly against high-level cleanup techniques, due to the inherent physical characteristics of modern storage media (e.g., SSD wear-leveling).\par
- \b Legal Basis:\b0  may assist the Customer in implementing storage-limitation and erasure obligations under applicable data-protection laws, subject to the Customer's own legal assessment.\par
- \b Scope Limitation:\b0  these features erase only the local endpoint; they do not erase offsite backups, cloud copies, or remote stores.\par
\pard\sb120\sa60\b 18.2 Access Segregation & Anti-Coercion Tools\b0\par
\pard\fi-240\li480\sb40 - \b Function:\b0  cryptographic access-segregation mechanisms, including multi-credential encrypted partitions and duress-activated protections, designed to separate classes of data under distinct authentication controls.\par
- \b Intended Use:\b0  to protect sensitive data against unauthorized access arising from \b physical coercion, theft, kidnapping, or extortion\b0 , and to reduce the risk of compelled disclosure of unrelated data sets to criminal actors. \b These tools are not represented as a means to defeat a lawful order; refusing a lawful decryption or production order may itself be an offence (e.g., under Section 69 of the IT Act, 2000), and the Customer remains solely responsible for lawful compliance.\b0\par
- \b Legal Basis:\b0  may assist the Customer in implementing reasonable security safeguards, subject to the Customer's own legal assessment.\par
